 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Depth Accuracy:** If the depth $d$ is poorly estimated, using a small $\alpha$ degrades rendering quality significantly [S1]. The parameter $\alpha$ must be set considering the accuracy of the depth images [S1].
-*   **Free Space Sampling:** Standard NeRF samples in free space and occluded regions, which may degrade quality. Surface-based sampling avoids this by restricting samples to the surface vicinity [S1].
+- **Depth Accuracy:** If the depth $d$ is poorly estimated, using a small $\alpha$ will degrade rendering quality because the sampling range will miss the actual surface [S1]. The parameter $\alpha$ must be set considering the accuracy of the depth images [S1].
+- **Free Space Sampling:** Standard NeRF samples in free space and occluded regions, which may degrade quality. This method avoids that by restricting sampling to the near-surface area [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** The sampling calculation is $O(N)$ per ray, identical to standard uniform or stratified sampling in terms of computational cost for generating coordinates. However, it requires an additional lookup or estimation step for depth $d$.
-*   **Performance:** Improves rendering accuracy (PSNR) by avoiding samples in irrelevant free space or occluded regions [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Prevents quality degradation from sampling in empty space; allows for denser sampling near the surface with fewer total samples if $\alpha$ is small [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Heavily dependent on the accuracy of the input depth images. Poor depth estimates lead to poor sampling locations [S1]. Requires depth images for training views and depth estimation/refinement for novel views [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When accurate depth information (from sensors or estimation) is available for the training set.
-*   **When not to use:** When depth accuracy is low or unavailable, as small $\alpha$ will degrade quality [S1].
+- **Time Complexity:** Reduces the number of unnecessary MLP evaluations in free space compared to uniform sampling, potentially improving rendering quality for a fixed computational budget or allowing faster convergence [S1].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for depth images or a point cloud generated from training data [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Prevents quality degradation caused by sampling in irrelevant regions (free space/occluded areas) [S1]. Can improve rendering performance without requiring a fine sampling process if the depth is accurate [S1].
+- **Limitations:** Heavily dependent on the accuracy of the depth estimates. Poor depth estimation with small $\alpha$ leads to poor results [S1].
+- **When to use:** When accurate depth information (from sensors or estimation) is available and the scene contains significant empty space or occlusions that standard NeRF samples inefficiently [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Surface-Based Sampling (also known as Near-Surface Sampling) was introduced in the paper "Improving Neural Radiance Fields Using Near-Surface Sampling with Point Cloud Generation" [S1]. The method addresses the inefficiency of standard NeRF, which samples uniformly along the entire ray length, including free space. By leveraging depth images to generate a point cloud and subsequently refining depth for novel views, the method focuses sampling near the object surface [S1].
+Surface-Based Sampling was introduced in the paper "Improving Neural Radiance Fields Using Near-Surface Sampling with Point Cloud Generation" [S1]. The method addresses the inefficiency of standard NeRF, which samples uniformly along rays regardless of whether the ray passes through empty space or the object [S1]. By leveraging depth images to generate a point cloud and subsequently refining depth for novel views, the method focuses the neural network's capacity on the object's surface [S1].
 
